The dyld pager releases its source page before it reads the other parts of a 16 KiB fixup page.

On a 4 KiB kernel a 16 KiB chained-fixup page is assembled from four source pages: the part being paged in, already
faulted, and the three others, read with dyld_pager_peek (both come from zzzz-sheng-ipados27-bringup.patch of the sheng
device repository, which this patch must follow). The source page of the part being paged in stayed busy, with its paging
reference on the file's VM object and the placeholder page in the copy object, while the others were read. A part that is
not resident is paged in from the file, and APFS pages a file with integrity verification in by the whole verification
block, with a UPL over all of its pages that waits uninterruptibly for every busy page of the block. The busy source page
is in that block, so the thread waited for itself in vm_object_upl_request (block hint 0x16), or for a thread whose page-in
waited for the page; the wait cannot be interrupted, so the process could not even exit. The paging reference it kept on
the file's object then held every later private mapping of that file in vm_object_copy_delayed (block hint 0x19): macOS
kernels wait there for paging to end.

The source page and the placeholder are now released right after the part is copied into the fixup buffer, the way the
end of the loop releases them, and the loop skips its release for such a part. The same release, for the same reason, is
in the shared region pager (native-4k-shared-region-slide.patch).

Not run on hardware.
--- a/osfmk/vm/vm_dyld_pager.c
+++ b/osfmk/vm/vm_dyld_pager.c
@@ -1330,8 +1330,32 @@
 			uint32_t part = (uint32_t)((page_off - base_off) / PAGE_SIZE);
 			uint64_t base_userVA = userVA - (uint64_t)(page_off - base_off);
 
-			/* The current page's source is already faulted and still mapped; fill the rest by peeking. */
+			/* The current page's source is already faulted and still mapped: copy it, then read the other parts. */
 			bcopy((const char *)src_vaddr, (char *)fixup_buffer + (vm_offset_t)part * PAGE_SIZE, PAGE_SIZE);
+
+			/*
+			 * The source page and its placeholder in the top object are released before the other parts are read,
+			 * the way the end of this loop releases them. A part that is not resident is paged in from the file, and
+			 * APFS pages a file with integrity verification in by the whole verification block, with a UPL over all
+			 * of its pages that waits, uninterruptibly, for every busy page of the block. A source page held busy
+			 * across the reads is such a page: the thread would wait in vm_object_upl_request for itself (or for a
+			 * thread whose page-in waits for this page), and its paging reference on the file's object would keep
+			 * every later private mapping of the file waiting in vm_object_copy_delayed.
+			 */
+			vm_object_lock(src_page_object);
+			vm_page_wakeup_done(src_page_object, src_page);
+			src_page = VM_PAGE_NULL;
+			vm_object_paging_end(src_page_object);
+			vm_object_unlock(src_page_object);
+			if (top_page != VM_PAGE_NULL) {
+				assert(VM_PAGE_OBJECT(top_page) == src_top_object);
+				vm_object_lock(src_top_object);
+				VM_PAGE_FREE(top_page);
+				vm_object_paging_end(src_top_object);
+				vm_object_unlock(src_top_object);
+				top_page = VM_PAGE_NULL;
+			}
+
 			retval = KERN_SUCCESS;
 			for (i = 0; i < mwli_page_size / PAGE_SIZE; ++i) {
 				if (i == part) {
@@ -1362,6 +1386,11 @@
 			}
 		}
 
+		if (src_page == VM_PAGE_NULL) {
+			/* a fixup page larger than a page: its source page is already released (above) */
+			continue;
+		}
+
 		assert(VM_PAGE_OBJECT(src_page) == src_page_object);
 		assert(src_page->vmp_busy);
 		assert(src_page_object->paging_in_progress > 0);
